00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:07.880
Every day your movement is tracked, your purchase is logged, your search is saved, and your face is scanned.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:13.780
Facial recognition technology is becoming more widespread daily. Privacy International has reported

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:19.480
24 countries have already implemented location tracking to help ensure compliance with quarantines.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.840
And if you were thinking that increased facemask usage might help protect your privacy,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.400
China's facial recognition algorithms have already figured out a way around them.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:35.100
In January 2020 the New York Times reported that a company called Clearview AI has created a database

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.160
that makes it possible to snap a photo of a stranger and reveal that person's identity. The technology

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.680
was developed using more than three billion images scraped from public social media accounts

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:52.960
by Australian Hoan Ton-that, whom the Huffington Post revealed has collaborated with anti-immigration

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:59.720
alt-right political operatives. Elements of Clearview AI are in use by more than 600 law enforcement agencies

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.800
in North America, including the FBI, Department of Homeland Security, and ICE.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.600
What if your software facilitates a world you didn't want to live in?

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:18.000
Well we're not going--we'll make sure that will happen. So can we resist the surveillance society? Should we?

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.920
Kate Rose says yes. I think you have a right to consent to how your information is used,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.320
especially if it's meant to be at some point used against you or used extra judicially.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.280
I am trying my best to explore these systems and see how we together can work to confound them,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.820
or to put data into them as we please. Rose is a designer, cybersecurity expert, and founder

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.980
of Adversarial Fashion, a line of surveillance resistant clothing such as masks

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.560
meant to block facial recognition cameras, and shirts patterned with fake license plates

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.660
meant to feed bad data into the automated license plate readers now ubiquitous in the US and Europe.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:59.080
I came up with a couple of different designs that not only do read effectively into license plate readers

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.220
but they gave me enough wiggle room
design wise to also make a pattern that is

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.560
aesthetic and fun to wear and something people would like to actually buy.

